 "question": "What is the difference between a shallow copy and a deep copy of a Python list?",
 "answer": "A shallow copy of a Python list, as demonstrated in [S1], makes both names refer to the same list, creating a new reference to the original list. This means that changes to the original list will be reflected in the copy. [S1] A deep copy, also as described in [S1], creates a new list with a new reference, and any nested objects are copied too, preventing the sharing of objects [S1] [S3].",
