   "kind": "document",
   "title": "Python Lecture Notes",
   "text": "up ages[\"Ali\"] raises KeyError when the key is missing, whereas ages.get(\"Sam\", 0) returns the default 0. The methods  keys(), values() and items() return views for iteration. Since Python 3.7 dictionaries preserve insertion order. Dictionary comprehensions such as {w: len(w) for w in words} are common. Copying matters: b = a makes both names refer to the same list, while b = a.copy() creates a shallow copy. A shallow copy still shares nested objects; copy.deepcopy copies them too.",
