   "kind": "document",
   "title": "Python Debugging Playbook",
   "text": "3. Lookup Errors Compared\nKeyError is raised when a dictionary key is missing. IndexError is raised when a sequence index is\nout of range. AttributeError is raised when an object has no attribute of that name.\nThe message 'NoneType' object has no attribute 'append' almost always means a variable holds\nNone instead of a list. The classic cause is writing nums = nums.sort(), because list.sort() sorts in\nplace and returns None. Use nums.sort() on its own line or sorted(nums) when a new list is\nneeded.\ndict.get(key, default) avoids a KeyError, while collections.defaultdict(list) creates a missing key with\nan empty list the first time it is accessed, which makes grouping code shorter.",
